%! Two kinds of equality.
%!
%! Nemo has two kinds of equality. `=` and `!=` check whether two terms
%! are the same. Joins use this equality, too, and it applies to any two
%! values. `==` and `!==` compare values, as `=` does in SPARQL: numbers
%! of different types, or dateTimes in different timezones, are equal if
%! they have the same value. A number and a string cannot be compared by
%! value, so `==` derives nothing for them, and Nemo warns about it.
%!
%! The example shows:
%! - how 1, 1.0d and 1f are three different terms but the same number,
%! - how two dateTimes can be the same point in time but different terms,
%! - how NaN is the same term as itself, but not equal to itself as a
%!   number.

@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

one(1) .
one(1.0d) .
one(1f) .
one("1") .

sameTerm(?x, ?y) :- one(?x), one(?y), ?x = ?y .
sameNumber(?x, ?y) :- one(?x), one(?y), ?x == ?y .

instant("2024-03-01T12:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime) .
instant("2024-03-01T13:00:00+01:00"^^xsd:dateTime) .

sameInstant(?x, ?y) :- instant(?x), instant(?y), ?x == ?y, ?x != ?y .

nan("NaN"^^xsd:double) .

nanIsSameTerm(yes) :- nan(?x), ?x = ?x .
nanIsEqual(yes) :- nan(?x), ?x == ?x .
nanIsNotEqual(yes) :- nan(?x), ?x !== ?x .

@output sameTerm, sameNumber, sameInstant, nanIsSameTerm, nanIsEqual, nanIsNotEqual .
